{"records":[{"id":"znt3g8r753","title":"MK2 phosphorylates RIPK1 to prevent TNF induced cell death. Jaco et al","description":"Original data of images and Western blot from the paper: MK2 phosphorylates RIPK1 to prevent TNF induced cell death. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-05-12T17:38:11.049Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/znt3g8r753","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/znt3g8r753"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Isabel","last_name":"Jaco","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alessandro","last_name":"Annibaldi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Najoua","last_name":"Lalaoui","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rebecca","last_name":"Wilson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tencho","last_name":"Tenev","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lucie","last_name":"Laurien","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chun","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kunzah","last_name":"Jamal","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sidonie ","last_name":"Wicky-John","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gianmaria","last_name":"Liccardi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Diep","last_name":"Chau","orcid":null},{"first_name":"James","last_name":"Murphy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gabriela","last_name":"Brumatti","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rebecca","last_name":"Feltham","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Manolis","last_name":"Pasparakis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"John","last_name":"Silke","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pascal","last_name":"Meier","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences","Molecular Biology","Cell Death"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"htg4vdkmvv","title":"Tetrahydrobenzimidazole TMQ0153 triggers apoptosis, autophagy and necroptosis crosstalk in chronic myeloid leukemia- Part 2 (Figure2, 3, 4, Supple 7,8))","description":"Abstract\nChronic myeloid leukemia (CML) results from a t(9;22) (q34; q11) translocation, also called Philadelphia chromosome (Ph). This reciprocal translocation causes a constitutively-activated tyrosine kinase BCR-ABL fusion gene. By comparing imatinib-sensitive and -resistant CML cell models, we investigated the molecular mechanisms by which tetrahydrobenzimidazole derivative TMQ0153 triggered caspase-dependent apoptosis at low concentrations accompanied by loss of mitochondrial membrane potential (MMP) and increase of cytosolic free Ca2+ levels. Interestingly, at higher concentrations, TMQ0153 induced necroptotic cell death with accumulation of ROS, both preventable by N-acetyl-L-cysteine (NAC) pretreatment. At necroptosis-inducing concentrations, we observed increased ROS and decreased ATP and GSH levels, concomitant with protective autophagy induction. Inhibitors such as bafilomycin A1 (baf-A1) and siRNA against beclin 1 abrogated autophagy, sensitized CML cells against TMQ0153 and enhanced necroptotic cell death. Importantly, TMQ153-induced necrosis led to cell surface exposure of calreticulin (CRT) and ERp57 as well as the release of extracellular ATP and high mobility group box (HMGB1) demonstrating the immunological cell death markers by TMQ0153. We validated the anti-cancer potential of TMQ0153 by in vivo inhibition of K562 microtumor formation in zebrafish. Taken together, our findings provide evidence that cellular stress and redox modulation by TMQ0153 concentration-dependently leads to different cell death modalities including controlled necrosis in CML cell models.\n\nConclusion: Our findings indicate that TMQ0153-induced ROS act as a rheostat determining the onset of apoptotic- or autophagy-related controlled necrotic cell death in CML.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-14T17:05:48.674Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/htg4vdkmvv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/htg4vdkmvv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sungmi","last_name":"Song","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z463p2gy9y","title":"Autoimmunity versus Apoptosis in some cases of Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia","description":"Introduce: Apoptosis, with roll in the scheduling of cell life, is a normal process different from cell necrosis, which occurs due\nto physical or chemical aggression. The decision to activate a suicide process is made based on intrinsic or extrinsic apoptotic\nmessages. The extrinsic ways which inducer apoptosis are ligands and cytokines for death receptors, (DR-Death receptors), being\non each cell surface. The intrinsic ways which inducer apoptosis come from the mitochondria or nucleus. Proapoptotic signals,\nwhich promote or cause apoptosis, participate in a cascade that leads to the culmination cleavage of a set of proteins, resulting in\nthe disassembly of the cells for apoptosis.\nAim: The scope of this review is to emphasise that massive apoptosis overloading the phagocytic capacity can trigger an\nautoimmune reaction through the presentation of nucleosomes to the immune system.\nMethods: A large cohort study of cases with the primary Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia, (CLL), examined samples from more\nthan 100 patients for response to MDM2 protein inhibition and found a direct correlation between wild-type P53 status and\nMDM2 inhibitor, Nutlin-3, which induced a cytotoxicity across various CLL subtypes. In experimental models, disrupting the MDM2–p53 protein interaction restored p53 function and sensitized tumours to chemotherapy or radiotherapy. This response was not presented in malignant B cells lymphocytes having the expression of\nZAP-70 receptor membrane, the expression of CD38 receptor with un-mutated immunoglobulin variable genes and with IGHV\nand mono-allelic ATM gene loss.\nResults: Pro-apoptotic signals apoptosis receptors, such as TNF-related apoptosis-inducing ligand, (TRAIL), Tumour Necrosis\nFactor, (TNF) and Fas receptor, also known as APO-1 or CD95 receptors are the key adaptor proteins transmitting apoptotic\nsignals mediated by the main death receptors, (DRs), known to induce and autophagy process.\nApoptosis is characterized by the apoptotic pathways, starting from the activation of the death receptor, (DRs), which is followed by a downstream signalling cascade including the involvement of mitochondria, subsequent caspase activation and DNA cleavage. However, pro-apoptotic signals apoptosis receptors, such as TNF-related apoptosis-inducing ligand, (TRAIL), Tumour Necrosis Factor, (TNF) and Fas receptor, also known as APO-1 or CD95 receptors are the key adaptor proteins transmitting apoptotic signals mediated by the main death receptors,\n(DRs). Pro-apoptotic signals, which promote or cause apoptosis, participate in a cascade and leads to the culmination cleavage ogene, which by its product, the p53 protein, inhibits the\nNk-beta nuclear factor.  Mutations of the P53 gene will lead to insufficient action of the p53 protein and trigger\nautoimmunity. Auto-reactive B and T cells, which escaped from natural apoptosis, might represent an additional necessary condition.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-17T13:26:56.933Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z463p2gy9y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z463p2gy9y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"AURELIAN","last_name":"UDRISTIOIU","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01q7jq182","name":"Unitatea Executiva Pentru Finantarea Invatamantului Superior a Cercetarii Dezvoltarii si Inovarii","grant_id":"U-1900-063Y4656"}],"categories":["Oncogenomics","Hematopathology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tk65df78y4","title":"RNA-sequencing data of YY1 knockdown (siYY1) in glioblastoma LN229 cells alongside control cell.","description":"To explore how YY1 regulates copper ionophore-induced cuproptosis in glioblastoma(GBM), we performed RNA sequencing and utilized YY1 knockdown (siYY1) in LN229 cells alongside control cells. To identify potential target genes that mediate copper ionophore-induced cell death.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-15T17:22:59.82Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tk65df78y4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tk65df78y4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yiju","last_name":"wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Glioma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"86yrzx7sfb","title":"Activation of clustered IFNγ target genes drives cohesin-controlled transcriptional memory. Siwek et al","description":"Cytokine activation of cells induces gene networks involved in inflammation and immunity. Transient gene activation can have a lasting impact even in the absence of ongoing transcription, known as long-term transcriptional memory. Here we explore the nature of the establishment and maintenance of IFNγ-induced priming of human cells. We find that while both ongoing transcription and local chromatin signatures are short-lived, the IFNγ-primed state stably propagates through at least 14 cell division cycles. Single cell analysis reveals that memory is manifested by an increased probability of primed cells to engage in target gene expression, correlating with the strength of initial gene activation. Further, we find that strongly memorized genes tend to reside in genomic clusters and that long-term memory of these genes is locally restricted by cohesin. We define the duration, stochastic nature and molecular mechanisms of IFNγ-induced transcriptional memory, relevant to understanding of enhanced innate immune signaling.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-26T12:32:46.061Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/86yrzx7sfb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/86yrzx7sfb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Wojciech","last_name":"Siwek","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sahar S.H. ","last_name":"Tehrani","orcid":null},{"first_name":"João F. ","last_name":"Mata","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lars E.T. ","last_name":"Jansen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Western Blot","Epigenetics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hv9zzc5ts2","title":"Herb-Derived Compounds From Radix Salviae Decoction (RSD) Modulated Cell Death Of Vascular Smooth Muscle Cells","description":"Research Hypothesis\nThe study hypothesized that herb-derived compounds from Radix Salviae Decoction (RSD) could modulate cell death of vascular smooth muscle cells (VSMC) through specific mechanisms. Specifically, it was hypothesized that these compounds might influence various forms of cell death, including pyroptosis, autophagy, and apoptosis, thereby affecting the function and survival of VSMC.\nData Description and Collection\nThe data was collected through a combination of network pharmacological analysis and in vitro experiments. The network pharmacological analysis involved identifying targets for three compounds from RSD (Tanshinone ⅡA (DST), Santalol (TXC), and Bornyl ester (LNZ)) and three types of cell death (pyroptosis, autophagy, and apoptosis) using literature and databases. A protein-protein interaction network was constructed using String, followed by Gene Ontology (GO) and Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) analyses to explore pathways affecting vascular smooth muscle cell death.\nIn vitro experiments were conducted on VSMC in serum-free medium (SFM) conditions. The effects of RSD-derived compounds on cell death were assessed using various methods, including measuring mRNA levels of specific genes (GSDMD, GSDME, IL-1β, ATG12), determining the LC3-II/I ratio, and using flow cytometry to assess early apoptotic cells. Additionally, the levels of cytokines (IL-6 and TNF-α) were measured, and the activity of cell signaling pathways (PI3K-AKT and NF-κB) was evaluated.\nData Interpretation\nThe data showed that treatment with RSD-derived compounds significantly promoted pyroptosis of VSMC, as evidenced by increased mRNA levels of GSDMD and GSDME, as well as increased IL-1β mRNA levels. These compounds also inhibited autophagy by reducing ATG12 expression and decreasing the LC3-II/I ratio. Furthermore, they decreased the proportion of early apoptotic cells and notably inhibited the production of IL-6 and TNF-α. In terms of cell signaling, the compounds suppressed the PI3K-AKT pathway while activating the NF-κB signaling pathway.\nNotable Findings\nThe notable findings include the identification of key proteins (CASP8, CASP3, TP53, JUN, and BAX) that play central roles in the protein interaction networks related to cell death modulation by RSD-derived compounds. These proteins are involved in various pathways affecting VSMC death, suggesting potential therapeutic targets for cardiovascular diseases.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-17T16:44:21.245Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hv9zzc5ts2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hv9zzc5ts2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qing","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Drug"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dmph2p5rcc","title":"SPATA2 regulates the activation of RIPK1 by modulating linear ubiquitination","description":"Stimulation of cells with TNFα leads to the formation of the TNF-R1 signaling complex (TNF-RSC) to mediate downstream cellular fate decision. Activation of the TNF-RSC is modulated by different types of ubiquitination and may lead to cell death, including apoptosis and necroptosis, in both RIPK1-dependent and RIPK1-independent manners. Spata2 (spermatogenesis-associated 2) is an adaptor protein recruited into the TNF-RSC to modulate the interaction between the linear ubiquitin chain assembly complex (LUBAC) and the deubiquitinase CYLD (cylindromatosis). However, the mechanism by which Spata2 regulates the activation of RIPK1 is unclear. Here, we report that Spata2-deficient cells show resistance to RIPK1-dependent apoptosis and necroptosis and are also partially protected against RIPK1-independent apoptosis. Spata2 deficiency promotes M1 ubiquitination of RIPK1 to inhibit RIPK1 kinase activity. Furthermore, we provide biochemical evidence for the USP domain of CYLD and the PUB domain of the SPATA2 complex preferentially deubiquitinating the M1 ubiquitin chain in vitro. Spata2 deficiency also promotes the activation of MKK4 and JNK and cytokine production independently of RIPK1 kinase activity. Spata2 deficiency sensitizes mice to systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS) induced by TNFα, which can be suppressed by RIPK1 inhibitor Nec-1s. Thus, Spata2 can regulate inflammatory response and cell death in both RIPK1-dependent and RIPK1-independent manners.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-08-02T17:40:36.615Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dmph2p5rcc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dmph2p5rcc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ran","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zdh5brnjv5","title":"SPATA2 regulates the activation of RIPK1 by modulating linear ubiquitination","description":"Stimulation of cells with TNFα leads to the formation of the TNF-R1 signaling complex (TNF-RSC) to mediate downstream cellular fate decision. Activation of the TNF-RSC is modulated by different types of ubiquitination and may lead to cell death, including apoptosis and necroptosis, in both RIPK1-dependent and RIPK1-independent manners. Spata2 (spermatogenesis-associated 2) is an adaptor protein recruited into the TNF-RSC to modulate the interaction between the linear ubiquitin chain assembly complex (LUBAC) and the deubiquitinase CYLD (cylindromatosis). However, the mechanism by which Spata2 regulates the activation of RIPK1 is unclear. Here, we report that Spata2-deficient cells show resistance to RIPK1-dependent apoptosis and necroptosis and are also partially protected against RIPK1-independent apoptosis. Spata2 deficiency promotes M1 ubiquitination of RIPK1 to inhibit RIPK1 kinase activity. Furthermore, we provide biochemical evidence for the USP domain of CYLD and the PUB domain of the SPATA2 complex preferentially deubiquitinating the M1 ubiquitin chain in vitro. Spata2 deficiency also promotes the activation of MKK4 and JNK and cytokine production independently of RIPK1 kinase activity. Spata2 deficiency sensitizes mice to systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS) induced by TNFα, which can be suppressed by RIPK1 inhibitor Nec-1s. Thus, Spata2 can regulate inflammatory response and cell death in both RIPK1-dependent and RIPK1-independent manners.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-08-02T17:39:12.052Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zdh5brnjv5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zdh5brnjv5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ran","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bnydp5xxw3","title":"Phytochemical profile and anti-inflammatory effect of Artemisia annua L. essential oil","description":"1.The cell viability of cells treated with different concentrations of AO did not affect cell viability. These data suggested that the AO was non-toxic to RAW264.7.\n2.The pre-treated with AO could alleviate the contents of NO, TNF-α,  IL-6 produced by LPS.\n3. AO showed significant inhibition on the ear edema.\n4. AO  could inhibited the rise of number of inflammatory cell induced by xylene and the  epidermal thickness.\n5. The level of TNF-α in the mice pretreated with AO  significantly decreased compared to the model group ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-15T18:10:45.295Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bnydp5xxw3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bnydp5xxw3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"pei","last_name":"zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zyh395xz2g","title":"Phytochemical profile and anti-inflammatory effect of Artemisia annua L. essential oil","description":"1.The cell viability of cells treated with different concentrations of AO did not affect cell viability. These data suggested that the AO was non-toxic to RAW264.7. 2.The pre-treated with AO could alleviate the contents of NO, TNF-α, IL-6 produced by LPS. 3. AO showed significant inhibition on the ear edema. 4. AO could inhibited the rise of number of inflammatory cell induced by xylene and the epidermal thickness. 5. The level of TNF-α in the mice pretreated with AO significantly decreased compared to the model group","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-25T06:50:59.398Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zyh395xz2g","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zyh395xz2g"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"pei","last_name":"zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Essential Oil","Antiinflammatory Activity"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":133486,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":154,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":133486,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":132744,"restricted":68,"embargoed":674},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2257},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":670},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":467},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":420},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":402},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":397},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":332},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":270},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":222}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":130857},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1901},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":132},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":75},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":56},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":510},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":356},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":356},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":304}]}}